# codebase-memory-mcp discovery rules for this repository.
#
# `src/vendored/` is a directory of **first-party source** that this plugin owns:
# it is a byte-derived copy of upstream's `ui-workspace` client, patched in place,
# reviewed and released as part of this package. The indexer's built-in skip list
# treats a directory named `vendored` as third-party code and drops the whole
# subtree — which is the opposite of what it is here. Roughly a fifth of the
# findings from the 2026-10-14 review live in that subtree, so leaving it out
# means the graph cannot answer questions about those files.
#
# The negation has to name the directory itself, not the files under it: a skipped
# parent is pruned before its children are visited, so `!src/vendored/**` alone
# would re-include nothing. Per the upstream discovery documentation, a
# `.cbmignore` negation can override the ordinary entries in the built-in skip
# list; only the four safety-core directories (`.git`, `node_modules`, and the two
# worktree directories) are immune to it.
#
# Keep this file even though it changes only indexing: it is what makes an index
# of this repository complete, and the next person to wonder why `src/vendored`
# is missing from the graph should find this comment instead of re-deriving it.
!src/vendored/

# `scripts/` is **first-party source** here too, and the built-in skip list drops it in every
# filtered mode (`fast` and `moderate` both report it under `excluded.dirs`, with identical node
# counts, so it is a skip-list rule rather than a per-mode pattern).
#
# That is 452 of the review's 641 findings — every `verify-*.mjs`, every `probe-*.mjs`, and
# `scripts/lib/` — invisible to the graph. The harness is not vendored or generated: it is the
# repository's own test surface, it is typed, and it is where the largest single batch of review
# findings lives. Same reasoning as `src/vendored/` above, and the same negation shape: the
# directory itself, because a skipped parent is pruned before its children are visited.
!scripts/
